🧠 Concept: How EKS Handles Storage
In Kubernetes, storage is managed using three key objects:

StorageClass — defines how storage should be provisioned (type, size, performance, etc.)

PersistentVolume (PV) — the actual storage resource in the cluster.

PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) — a request made by a pod for storage.

In EKS, the storage is backed by AWS storage services such as:

EBS (Elastic Block Store) — for single node (pod) storage.

EFS (Elastic File System) — for shared file storage (multiple pods).

S3 — for object storage (not used directly as PV/PVC, but via CSI drivers or apps).

⚙️ Real-Time Example — Using AWS EBS with StorageClass and PVC
Let’s say you have a MySQL database pod running in EKS.
You want its data to be stored persistently (so data isn’t lost when the pod restarts).

We’ll use:

AWS EBS volume (through CSI driver)

StorageClass to define how the EBS volume should be created

PVC to request that storage

Pod/Deployment to use that PVC

🔹 Step 1: Install the AWS EBS CSI Driver
EKS doesn’t create EBS volumes automatically — we need the EBS CSI driver.

You can install it easily using:

eksctl create addon --name aws-ebs-csi-driver --cluster my-eks-cluster --region ap-south-1
🔹 Step 2: Create a StorageClass
# storageclass.yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: gp2-storage
provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
parameters:
  type: gp2   # General Purpose SSD
reclaimPolicy: Retain
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
Explanation:

provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com → tells Kubernetes to use AWS EBS CSI driver.

type: gp2 → EBS volume type.

reclaimPolicy: Retain → even if PVC is deleted, EBS volume is retained (for data safety).

WaitForFirstConsumer → volume will be created only when the pod is scheduled (to ensure it’s in the correct AZ).

🔹 Step 3: Create a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)
# pvc.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: mysql-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce  # Only one pod can mount it at a time (EBS limitation)
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi
  storageClassName: gp2-storage
Explanation:

ReadWriteOnce → EBS can be mounted to only one node at a time.

storage: 5Gi → the volume size requested.

storageClassName → matches our StorageClass.

🔹 Step 4: Create a Deployment that Uses the PVC
# mysql-deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: mysql
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: mysql
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: mysql
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: mysql
          image: mysql:8
          env:
            - name: MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD
              value: mypassword
          ports:
            - containerPort: 3306
          volumeMounts:
            - name: mysql-storage
              mountPath: /var/lib/mysql
      volumes:
        - name: mysql-storage
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: mysql-pvc
Explanation:

The container mounts the storage at /var/lib/mysql.

Data stored here will persist even if the pod restarts.

🔹 Step 5: Apply All Files
kubectl apply -f storageclass.yaml
kubectl apply -f pvc.yaml
kubectl apply -f mysql-deployment.yaml
🔹 Step 6: Verify
kubectl get sc
kubectl get pvc
kubectl get pv
kubectl get pods
You’ll see:

The PVC gets Bound to a PV (EBS volume created in AWS).

The Pod is Running and using that volume.

🧩 Real-Life DevOps Use Case
In a production EKS setup, DevOps engineers use:

EBS volumes for databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.)

EFS for shared storage (multiple pods reading/writing files, like NFS)

S3 for backups or logs (accessed using SDKs, not as PV/PVC directly)

Example:

Jenkins running in EKS → stores build data in EFS (shared storage)

MySQL or MongoDB pod → uses EBS for persistent volume

Application logs → shipped to S3 via FluentBit

