⚙️ Meaning of accessModes
In a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC),
the accessModes field tells Kubernetes how the storage can be mounted and used by pods.

Here are the possible access modes 👇

Access Mode	Short Name	Meaning	AWS Backend Supported
ReadWriteOnce	RWO	Volume can be mounted as read/write by only one node	✅ EBS
ReadOnlyMany	ROX	Volume can be read-only by many nodes	❌ (Not supported by EBS)
ReadWriteMany	RWX	Volume can be read/write by many pods across multiple nodes	✅ EFS
📘 Example with ReadWriteOnce (RWO)
If your PVC has:

accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
That means:

The EBS volume can be attached to only one EC2 node (worker node) at a time.

Only pods on that node can read/write to it.

If another pod is scheduled on a different node, it cannot access that same EBS volume.

This is an EBS limitation, not Kubernetes’ —
EBS volumes can only be attached to a single EC2 instance at a time.

🧩 Now Your Question:
Suppose the deployment contains multiple pods like 10, then why other pod will write or read a data from storage in AWS?

Perfect — let’s visualize that 👇

🖼️ Example: 10-Replica Deployment using EBS (ReadWriteOnce)
Let’s say you have:

replicas: 10
and all 10 pods use the same PVC (with ReadWriteOnce).

Here’s what happens:

Kubernetes tries to attach the EBS volume (from the PVC) to the node running the first pod.

The first pod runs fine and uses the volume.

When Kubernetes tries to schedule the other 9 pods (on other nodes), it fails —
because EBS volume can’t be attached to more than one node.

So you’ll see pods in ContainerCreating or Pending state —
they can’t mount the same volume.

✅ Solution:
If each pod needs its own storage (like each MySQL replica),
create multiple PVCs, one per pod.

🖼️ Example: 10 Pods Need Shared Storage
If you want all pods to read/write to the same shared storage,
then EBS won’t work.

Instead, use Amazon EFS — a shared network file system.

EFS supports:

accessModes:
  - ReadWriteMany
With EFS, all 10 pods can mount the same volume (from different nodes)
and read/write to it simultaneously.

That’s ideal for:

Jenkins shared workspace

WordPress shared uploads

Web servers sharing logs or content

🔍 Summary for DevOps Engineers
Storage Type	Access Mode	Mounts to Multiple Pods?	Use Case
EBS	ReadWriteOnce	❌ Only one pod/node	Databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL)
EFS	ReadWriteMany	✅ Many pods/nodes	Shared files, Jenkins, WordPress
S3	(Not PVC-based)	✅ Many apps (via SDK)	Backups, Logs, Artifacts
🧠 Real-Life Analogy
Think of it like this:

EBS = A USB drive plugged into one computer — only that computer can use it.

EFS = A network drive (NAS) — many computers can access it at the same time.

