🧩 Step 1: Create an Amazon EFS File System
You can do this manually from AWS Console or AWS CLI.

Using AWS Console:

Go to EFS → Create file system

Choose your VPC (same VPC as your EKS cluster)

Keep defaults for Performance mode and Throughput

Enable mount targets in all subnets where EKS worker nodes run

After creation, note down the File system ID, e.g.:

fs-0a1b2c3d4e5f6g7h
🧩 Step 2: Install the AWS EFS CSI Driver
This allows Kubernetes to mount EFS volumes.

Run:

eksctl create addon --name aws-efs-csi-driver --cluster my-eks-cluster --region ap-south-1
Or using Helm:

helm repo add aws-efs-csi-driver https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/aws-efs-csi-driver/
helm install aws-efs-csi-driver aws-efs-csi-driver/aws-efs-csi-driver \
  --namespace kube-system \
  --set controller.serviceAccount.create=false \
  --set controller.serviceAccount.name=efs-csi-controller-sa
🧩 Step 3: Create an EFS Access Point (Recommended)
In AWS Console → EFS → your file system → Access Points → Create access point

Choose your file system

Give a name (e.g., /shared-data)

Use default permissions (owner: 1000:1000, path: /data)

You’ll get an Access Point ID, e.g.:

fsap-0abcdef123456789
🧩 Step 4: Create a StorageClass for EFS
# storageclass-efs.yaml
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: efs-sc
provisioner: efs.csi.aws.com
reclaimPolicy: Retain
volumeBindingMode: Immediate
Explanation:

provisioner: efs.csi.aws.com → uses EFS CSI driver

reclaimPolicy: Retain → keeps EFS data even if PVC is deleted

Immediate → volume binds right away (no need to wait for pod scheduling)

🧩 Step 5: Create a PersistentVolume (PV)
Unlike EBS, EFS doesn’t auto-provision volumes per claim —
so we define a PV that points to the EFS filesystem.

# pv-efs.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: efs-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 5Gi   # Logical size (EFS scales automatically)
  volumeMode: Filesystem
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteMany   # <-- Important: shared access!
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: efs-sc
  csi:
    driver: efs.csi.aws.com
    volumeHandle: fs-0a1b2c3d4e5f6g7h::fsap-0abcdef123456789
Explanation:

volumeHandle:
Combine your EFS File System ID and Access Point ID
→ fs-xxxx::fsap-xxxx

ReadWriteMany: allows multiple pods on different nodes to access the same data.

🧩 Step 6: Create a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)
# pvc-efs.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: efs-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteMany
  storageClassName: efs-sc
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi
Explanation:

ReadWriteMany → multiple pods can mount this PVC at once.

PVC will bind to the PV we created above.

